  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The empirical evidence is drawn from a single, highly specific political and cultural context in Yogyakarta, which may limit its direct applicability to heritage-led gentrification in non-monarchical systems.",
      "what": "This study analyzes the reconstruction of the fortress around the Yogyakarta Kraton in Indonesia, a project tied to a UNESCO World Heritage designation. Using a multi-method approach, it examines how the state-led construction of heritage facilitates tourism and gentrification while legitimizing the displacement of local residents.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that state-led gentrification and heritagization can function as tools of political theology, where the physical reconstruction of urban space serves to glorify state power and reinforce the dual cultural and governmental authority of the ruling Sultanate."
